To verify the probe you should bring a **specific‑conductance (SC) standard** that brackets the conductivity you expect in the field. USGS guidance says you should “choose the SC standard(s) nearest to and/or that bracket(s) the expected measurement or range. To field check the calibration, select additional standard(s) that bracket the expected sample SC.”【7†"Choose the SC standard(s) nearest to and/or that bracket(s) the expected measurement or range. To field check the calibration, select additional standard(s) that bracket the expected sample SC."】

Because your water is about 700 µS/cm in summer, a good practice is to carry at least two standards—one a little below (e.g., 500 µS/cm) and one a little above (e.g., 1000 µS/cm). If a 700 µS/cm standard is available, that can be used as the primary check.

In addition, bring:

* **Deionized water** (maximum conductivity 1 µS/cm) for rinsing the probe between checks【7†"Deionized water Maximum conductivity of 1 μS/cm (ASTM Type 2) for rinsing sensors"}  
* **NIST‑traceable temperature reference** (calibration thermometer) because temperature compensation affects the reading【7†"Calibration thermometer and barometer NIST‑certified or NIST‑traceable"}  

Using the standards that bracket 700 µS/cm and the rinse water will let you confirm that the probe is still within the acceptable tolerance (±3 % for measurements > 100 µS/cm) without needing a full recalibration.
